    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "2a75a19a_73721064",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_gpio.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 47,
      "author": {
        "id": 1308749
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-25T17:35:44Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "The use of indexes seems pretty fragile - your `int_config` and `spec` arrays are different sizes for example.\n\nI think it would be better to either pass `enum power_signal` everywhere.  This wastes some space as the GPIO, VW, and external drivers all have to allocate sparse arrays.\n\nYou could also have each driver register a list of power signals with `signal_interface.c`.  Then `signal_interface.c` should pass a pointer to the sub-driver\u0027s power signal structure.\n\nSomething like\n```\nvoid power_signal_register(enum power_signal, void *drv_data, ...)\n````\n\n`signal_interface.c` would statically allocate an array that can hold all possible power signal names.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 47,
        "startChar": 33,
        "endLine": 47,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "bd7c7ba41d3e9171aafeccf28e9b8a47b832ee54",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "aff68d5f_961cfd87",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_gpio.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 47,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-26T00:09:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "So agree that splitting the input and out GPIOs was a little too fancy, and now I\u0027ve combined it. The only downside is that some space is lost (interrupt callback block for output GPIOs), but that\u0027s not too bad.\n\nThe use of a separate index for each of the power signal sources is internally hidden, and driven from the config, so",
      "parentUuid": "2a75a19a_73721064",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 47,
        "startChar": 33,
        "endLine": 47,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "bd7c7ba41d3e9171aafeccf28e9b8a47b832ee54",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "a25fb710_bc670b99",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_gpio.c",
        "patchSetId": 12
      },
      "lineNbr": 47,
      "author": {
        "id": 1281040
      },
      "writtenOn": "2022-02-26T00:17:02Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "Sorry, unfinished response.\n\nSo the idea is to use enum power_signal everywhere in the main power sequence code, and with any external code (e.g board level functions) that interact with this code.\nWhat I\u0027m trying to do is decouple the method of access of the signal from this common code, and internally in the access code (e.g signal_interface.c signal_gpio.c and signal_vw.c) whatever it uses to map the power_signal value is private data - this code really doesn\u0027t have to deal with enum power_signal at all, and in fact if it had to, it would be violating some layering.\nThe fact that the config is built from DTS also means the mapping is automatically created safely (theoretically :-)",
      "parentUuid": "aff68d5f_961cfd87",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 47,
        "startChar": 33,
        "endLine": 47,
        "endChar": 46
      },
      "revId": "bd7c7ba41d3e9171aafeccf28e9b8a47b832ee54",
      "serverId": "3ce6091f-6c88-37e8-8c75-72f92ae8dfba"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "b91f9531_8cf22a38",
        "filename": "zephyr/subsys/ap_pwrseq/signal_gpio.c",
